# Spec 020 — Monthly-Payment Budget

Status: Draft
Owner: <you>
Related: 001 R4 (budget de-emphasis — the pipeline this feeds), 009 R5
(URL state), 014 (price-to-income — the ratio this complements), 015 R2
(budget input polish), 019 (matches shortlist — consumes the derived
budget unchanged). Named as the candidate follow-up in 014 and 019.

## Overview

The budget is a purchase price, but most people know their monthly
ceiling, not a sticker price. This spec lets the user state the budget
as a monthly payment — with a down-payment percentage and an interest
rate — and converts it to a maximum home price with the standard
amortization formula plus a flat tax-and-insurance allowance. The
derived price feeds the existing pipeline (opacity, hatch, legend,
matches, "under/over budget" badge) unchanged, so the feature is a
pure client-side input mode: no backend changes, no new dependencies,
no additional Mapbox spend.

## Requirements

- **R1 — Conversion math.** A pure function maps (monthly payment,
  down-payment %, annual rate %, term years, annual tax+insurance % of
  price) to a maximum price: `price = payment / (L·A + T/12)` where
  `L = 1 − down/100` is the financed fraction, `A = r / (1 − (1+r)^−n)`
  is the per-dollar monthly amortization factor (`r` = monthly rate,
  `n` = months; `A = 1/n` when the rate is zero), and `T` is the annual
  tax+insurance fraction. The inverse (monthly payment for a price)
  exists for display. Defaults: 30-year term, 20 % down, 6.5 % rate,
  1.5 % tax+insurance, all overridable except term and tax+insurance
  (kept as documented constants for the MVP). Results round to the
  nearest $1,000; non-positive payments yield 0 (no budget).
- **R2 — Input mode.** The budget control gains a two-way toggle,
  "Home price" | "Monthly payment". Price mode is today's input,
  untouched. Payment mode shows a monthly-payment input plus compact
  down-payment (%) and rate (%) fields, and a live line "≈ max price
  $X · assumes 30-yr, 1.5 %/yr tax + insurance". The derived price
  becomes the app's budget for every existing consumer.
- **R3 — URL state.** Payment mode round-trips as `pay=`, with `down=`
  and `rate=` present only when they differ from the defaults; price
  mode keeps `budget=`. A URL carrying `pay` opens in payment mode.
  Invalid values (non-numeric, out of range: down 0–99, rate 0–30) are
  dropped silently like every other param.
- **R4 — Detail-panel echo.** In payment mode the ZIP detail panel's
  budget badge appends the ZIP's estimated monthly payment under the
  same assumptions (e.g. "Under budget by $40,000 · ≈ $3,180/mo"), so
  the user can compare in the unit they think in.

## Non-functional / Constraints

- The math lives in `frontend/src/lib/affordability.ts` with unit tests
  against hand-checked values; components stay thin.
- No backend changes; `budget` reaching every existing consumer is
  still a number of dollars.
- The toggle follows the existing segmented-control (`.switcher`) and
  touch-target rules (010 R5).

## Acceptance Criteria

- [ ] Payment mode, $3,000/mo, 20 % down, 6.5 % → max price $476,000
      (hand-checked: A = 0.0063205, L·A + T/12 = 0.0063064,
      3000 / 0.0063064 = 475,706 → nearest $1k); the map hatches ZIPs
      above it and the matches list respects it exactly as a typed
      price would.
- [ ] Changing the rate or down payment re-derives the price live;
      switching back to price mode restores the previously typed price.
- [ ] `?pay=3000&rate=7` opens in payment mode with those values and
      the derived budget applied; `?budget=600000` still works.
- [ ] Clicking a ZIP in payment mode shows the ≈ $/mo echo on the
      badge; in price mode it does not.
- [ ] `npm run test` green, including new unit tests for the math and
      component tests for the toggle and URL codec.

## Out of Scope (explicit)

- Income-based (DTI) budgeting, PMI, HOA dues, per-state property-tax
  rates — the allowance is one documented national constant.
- Rate lookups or live mortgage data.
- Persisting assumptions beyond the URL.

## Verification notes

R1 → affordability unit tests (hand-checked values, zero rate, zero
